---
import FeaturePage from '../layouts/FeaturePage.astro';
import FeatureActions from '../components/FeatureActions.astro';
import Showcase from '../components/Showcase.astro';
import ProductMedia from '../components/ProductMedia.astro';
import variablesCrop from '../assets/gql-variables-form-crop.webp';
import operations from '../assets/gql_multi_queries-detail.webp';
import variables from '../assets/gql_var_form-detail.webp';
import schema from '../assets/gql_schema-detail.webp';
import autocomplete from '../assets/gql_autocomplete-detail.webp';
---

<FeaturePage
  title="Purr — GraphQL Client for macOS"
  description="A GraphQL client for macOS with schema-aware editing, autocomplete, multiple operations, structured variables, and a full schema explorer."
  name="GraphQL client"
  variant="graphql-page"
>
  <header class="container feature-hero graphql-hero">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow magenta">GRAPHQL CLIENT / MACOS</p>
      <h1>
        GraphQL deserves more than a <span class="muted">JSON textbox.</span>
      </h1>
      <FeatureActions docs="/docs/graphql/" label="Read GraphQL docs" />
    </div>
    <div class="feature-prose">
      <p>I have spent a lot of time working with GraphQL APIs.</p>
      <p>
        Browser-based GraphQL tools are great when everything is simple. But eventually the schema
        is behind authentication, requests need custom headers, you want to keep queries around, or
        you are switching between several operations and variable sets.
      </p>
      <p>
        At that point I usually ended up moving back into a general HTTP client. The problem is that
        GraphQL support in many HTTP clients still feels like a special body type for a POST
        request.
      </p>
      <p class="feature-emphasis">Purr treats GraphQL as its own workflow.</p>
    </div>
  </header>
  <section class="container graphql-workbench" aria-label="Explore the GraphQL client">
    <Showcase
      id="graphql-client"
      label="GraphQL workflows"
      variant="graphql-showcase"
      items={[
        {
          label: 'Operations',
          image: operations,
          alt: 'Purr GraphQL document with Cases and CaseDetails operations and individual send controls',
          description: 'Multiple operations. Run the one you need.',
        },
        {
          label: 'Variables',
          image: variables,
          alt: 'Purr structured GraphQL variables with a CaseStatus enum selector, pageSize field, and Form or JSON switch',
          description: 'Typed variables. A form when you want it, JSON when you need it.',
        },
        {
          label: 'Schema',
          image: schema,
          alt: 'Purr full GraphQL schema explorer showing queries, types, field arguments and descriptions',
          description: 'Explore the schema without losing your request.',
        },
        {
          label: 'Autocomplete',
          image: autocomplete,
          alt: 'Purr GraphQL editor suggesting schema fields including typename, assignee and author',
          description: 'Schema-aware suggestions as you write.',
        },
      ]}
    />
  </section>
  <section class="container graphql-chapters">
    <article>
      <span class="chapter-number">01 / EDITOR</span>
      <div>
        <h2>Write queries with the schema beside you.</h2>
        <p>
          Once Purr connects to the schema, the editor can use it for field autocomplete, argument
          suggestions, type information, hover documentation, and operation awareness.
        </p>
        <p>
          You do not have to constantly jump between the query and a separate schema page just to
          remember what a field accepts.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>
    <article>
      <span class="chapter-number">02 / OPERATIONS</span>
      <div>
        <h2>Choose the operation you actually want to run.</h2>
        <p>A GraphQL document often contains more than one query or mutation.</p>
        <p>
          Purr detects the operations in the document and lets you send the one you want directly,
          instead of forcing you to constantly rewrite the body or maintain separate copies of the
          same document.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </section>
  <section class="container variables-story">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow magenta">VARIABLES DO NOT HAVE TO BE RAW JSON</p>
      <h2>
        Fill the variables.
        <br />
        <span class="muted">Don’t rewrite the object.</span>
      </h2>
      <p>
        I never liked having to manually edit the object every time I wanted to change one value.
      </p>
      <p>
        Purr can render GraphQL variables as a structured form based on the operation. Required
        values are visible, scalar types are clear, enums can be selected, and you can change a
        value without touching the JSON structure.
      </p>
      <p>If you prefer JSON, it is still there.</p>
    </div>
    <ProductMedia
      src={variablesCrop}
      alt="GraphQL variables form with the required CaseStatus enum expanded to show PENDING, IN_PROGRESS and CLOSED, a pageSize value of 5, and the Form / JSON switch"
      caption="Choose a status, set the page size, or switch to JSON."
      class="variables-form-preview"
    />
  </section>
  <section class="container schema-story">
    <p class="eyebrow magenta">THE SCHEMA IS NOT HIDDEN IN A SIDEBAR</p>
    <h2>Browse the API properly.</h2>
    <p>
      Purr has a full schema explorer rather than treating introspection as a small helper panel.
      For a type, you can move through its fields and understand how the schema is connected without
      losing your current request.
    </p>
    <ul class="schema-types">
      {['Queries', 'Mutations', 'Types', 'Fields', 'Arguments', 'Descriptions', 'Nested types'].map(
        (type) => (
          <li>{type}</li>
        ),
      )}
    </ul>
    <div class="schema-workflow">
      <div>
        <h3>Documentation should lead somewhere.</h3>
        <p>
          If you find a query or mutation while browsing the schema, you can use it as the starting
          point for a request instead of manually rebuilding the operation in the editor.
        </p>
        <p>The schema is not only reference documentation. It is part of the request workflow.</p>
      </div>
      <ol class="feature-flow vertical-flow" aria-label="Start a GraphQL request from the schema">
        <li>Schema field</li>
        <li>Create operation</li>
        <li>Fill variables</li>
        <li>Send</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container feature-editorial">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow magenta">AUTHENTICATION STAYS IN THE WORKSPACE</p>
      <h2>GraphQL does not live in an isolated browser tab.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-prose">
      <p>
        If the schema requires authentication or custom headers, it uses the same workspace concepts
        as the rest of Purr.
      </p>
      <p>
        That means your GraphQL workflow can use the same environments, authentication, variables,
        and <a href="/local-first-api-client/">local workspace</a> as your HTTP requests.
      </p>
      <p>No browser extension just to inject a header.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container graphql-loop">
    <p class="eyebrow magenta">BUILT FOR GRAPHQL</p>
    <h2>GraphQL is a first-class document type in Purr.</h2>
    <p>
      The goal is not to reproduce every specialized GraphQL IDE feature. It is to make the common
      GraphQL workflow feel native inside an API client.
    </p>
    <ol class="workflow-checklist">
      {[
        'Connect the schema',
        'Write or browse an operation',
        'Fill variables',
        'Send one of several operations',
        'Inspect the response',
        'Keep the request in your workspace',
      ].map((step, i) => (
        <li>
          <span>0{i + 1}</span>
          {step}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </section>
  <section class="container feature-end">
    <h2>Use GraphQL without treating it like a POST body.</h2>
    <p>
      Schema-aware editing, proper variables, multiple operations, and a full explorer live in the
      same workspace as the rest of your API requests.
    </p>
    <FeatureActions docs="/docs/graphql/" label="GraphQL documentation" />
  </section>
</FeaturePage>
